    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 261637\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n940 AM PDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Southwesterly breezes and seasonable temperatures will give way\n  to cooler temperatures and increased precipitation chances\n  Sunday through Tuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Friday.\n\nSeasonable temperatures are on tap for the region today, with breezy \nsouthwesterly winds picking up across the region as an upper-level \ntrough begins to develop off the coast of the Pacific Northwest. \nThis trough will begin to dig along the coast on Sunday, which will \nbring cooler temperatures and gusty southwesterly winds to the area \non Sunday. While this trough digs along the coast, Hurricane Polo \nwill continue to approach the Baja Peninsula, pushing tropical \nmoisture into the Southwestern US ahead of it. This increase in \nmoisture will bring increased precipitation chances to southern \nNevada, southeastern California, and northwestern Arizona late \nSunday through Tuesday. Things like precipitation totals and the \nexact locations of showers and thunderstorms remain uncertain due to \nuncertainty regarding the timing of the aforementioned trough and \nwhere it intercepts the moisture. If the trough is positioned \nslightly further west, we will see more moisture advected into the \narea ahead of the trough, which will result in the potential for \nmore widespread precipitation and higher precipitation totals. \nShould the trough intercept the moisture further to the east, less \nmoisture will be available and showers and thunderstorms will be \nmore limited to areas around the low and out ahead of the trough \naxis. This trough will clear out of the area on Wednesday, with \nridging setting up across the Southwestern US behind it. As a \nresult, weather during the second half of the will be drier and \nwarmer.\n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Breezy \nsouthwesterly winds will increase through the remainder of the \nmorning hours and continue thru the early evening. Gusts will \nsubside overnight before returning Sunday afternoon. Mid and high \nclouds will increase overnight, but remain generally AOA 12kft AGL.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Gusty south-\nsouthwesterly winds expected across the Las Vegas Valley and \nColorado River Valley thru the afternoon with speeds between 20 and \n25 kts. KBIH will gust from the south-southeast to 25 kts and KDAG \nwill gust from the west to 25 kts. Increasing mid and high clouds \nexpected overnight...mainly across the Mojave Desert and northwest \nArizona. &&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Stessman\nAVIATION...Soulat/CO\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
